  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Transitive and intransitive verbs",
      "summary": "A transitive verb takes an object; an intransitive verb does not.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A transitive verb takes an object. An intransitive verb does not."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "She **reads** a book. (transitive)",
                "He **sleeps**. (intransitive)",
                "I **stopped** the car. The car **stopped**."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Many English verbs can be both. A dictionary marks them as vt or vi. Some verbs take two objects: She gave him a gift."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>transitive</td><td><strong>He opened the door.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>intransitive</td><td><strong>The door opened.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>two objects</td><td><strong>I gave her a book.</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
